    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the value of 'b + m', we can take advantage of the fact that both lines intersect at a point (6, 10). This means that when we substitute 'x' and 'y' to be 6 and 10 in both equations, they should result in the same 'b' and 'm'.\n\n1. Start with the equation of the first line:\n   \\[\n   y = mx + 4\n   \\]\n   Substitute the given point (6, 10) into this equation to solve for 'm':\n   \\[\n   10 = 6m + 4\n   \\]\n   Solving this equation for 'm':\n   \\[\n   10 - 4 = 6m\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 = 6m\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   m = 1\n   \\]\n\n2. Next, use the value of 'm' to find 'b' in the equation of the second line:\n   \\[\n   y = 3x + b\n   \\]\n   Substitute the given point (6, 10) into this equation to solve for 'b':\n   \\[\n   10 = 3(6) + b\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   10 = 18 + b\n   \\]\n   Solving this equation for 'b':\n   \\[\n   10 - 18 = b\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = -8\n   \\]\n\n3. Finally, calculate 'b + m' using the values found for 'b' and 'm':\n   \\[\n   b + m = -8 + 1 = -7\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\nThe value of 'b + m' is $\\boxed{-7}$.",
